<p>Mathematician Giuseppe Peano created an auxiliary language to facilitate communication between people of different nations. Latin already served as an international language, he reasoned; &#8220;Latino sine flexione&#8221; could retain that language&#8217;s vocabulary but simplify its grammar as much as possible. In a 1908 speech announcing the idea, he began speaking in classical Latin and adopted each simplification as he described it until he was speaking in the new language. Here&#8217;s the Lord&#8217;s Prayer:</p>
<p>Nostro patre, qui es in caelos,<br />
que tuo nomine fi sanctificato;<br />
que tuo regno adveni;<br />
que tuo voluntate es facto<br />
sicut in caelo et in terra.<br />
Da hodie ad nos nostro pane quotidiano,<br />
et remitte ad nos nostro debitos,<br />
sicut et nos remitte ad nostro debitores.<br />
Et non induce nos in tentatione,<br />
sed libera nos ab malo.<br />
Amen.</p>
<p>In the end it was not widely adopted, but most of Peano&#8217;s books and papers are written in his proposed language.</p>
